---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `local ship = { name = "Skynest", crew = 4, online = true }

print(ship.name)
print(ship["crew"])
print(ship.captain)`;
const Figure1Out = `Skynest
4
nil`;

const Figure2 = `local ship = { name = "Skynest", crew = 4, online = true }

for key, value in pairs(ship) do
  print(key, value)
end`;
const Figure2Out = `online\ttrue
crew\t4
name\tSkynest`;

const Figure3 = `local ship = {
  name = "Skynest",
  bay = { docked = 2, capacity = 6 },
}

print(ship.bay.docked)
print(ship.bay.capacity)

ship.bay.docked = 3
print(ship.bay.docked)`;
const Figure3Out = `2
6
3`;

const Figure4 = `local ship = { name = "Skynest" }

ship.charge = 98
print(ship.charge)

ship.charge = nil
print(ship.charge)`;
const Figure4Out = `98
nil`;

const Figure5 = `shipName = "Skynest"

print(_G.shipName)
print(_G["shipName"])`;
const Figure5Out = `Skynest
Skynest`;

const Task1 = `-- main.lua - Skynest ship record

local ship = {
  name = "Skynest",
  crew = 4,
  online = true,
}

print(ship.name)
print(ship.crew)
print(ship.captain)`;
const Task1Out = `Skynest
4
nil`;

const Task2 = `-- main.lua - Skynest ship record

local ship = {
  name = "Skynest",
  crew = 4,
  online = true,
  bay = { docked = 2, capacity = 6 },
}

print(ship.name)
print(ship.crew)
print(ship.captain)

print("Docked:", ship.bay.docked, "of", ship.bay.capacity)

ship.bay.docked = ship.bay.docked + 1
print("Docked:", ship.bay.docked, "of", ship.bay.capacity)`;
const Task2Out = `Skynest
4
nil
Docked:\t2\tof\t6
Docked:\t3\tof\t6`;

const Task3 = `-- main.lua - Skynest ship record

local ship = {
  name = "Skynest",
  crew = 4,
  online = true,
  bay = { docked = 2, capacity = 6 },
}

print(ship.name)
print(ship.crew)
print(ship.captain)

print("Docked:", ship.bay.docked, "of", ship.bay.capacity)

ship.bay.docked = ship.bay.docked + 1
print("Docked:", ship.bay.docked, "of", ship.bay.capacity)

ship.charge = 98
print("Charge:", ship.charge)

ship.online = nil

local keys = {}
for key in pairs(ship) do
  table.insert(keys, key)
end
table.sort(keys)

for _, key in ipairs(keys) do
  print(key, type(ship[key]))
end`;

const Checkpoint = `Skynest
4
nil
Docked:\t2\tof\t6
Docked:\t3\tof\t6
Charge:\t98
bay\ttable
charge\tnumber
crew\tnumber
name\tstring`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/lua-programming/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.10: Tables as Records</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Concept -->
        <section id="concept">
          <h2>The Idea</h2>

          <p>
            Same table, different job. Instead of numbering the slots, you{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">name</b> them &mdash; and now one table can
            describe a whole thing rather than a sequence of things.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure1}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure1Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <CodeSnippet
              code="ship.name"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code={`ship["name"]`}
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            are the same thing written two ways. The dot is shorthand and only
            works for names you could use as a variable; the brackets work for
            anything, which is what you need when the key is <i>in</i> a
            variable:{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="ship[whichField]"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <p>
            A key that was never set is{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="nil"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />. By now you should expect that, and you should also expect that
            nobody warns you. A typo in a field name behaves exactly like a typo
            in a variable name.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">There is no difference between the two uses</h3>

          <p>
            This is worth stating plainly, because it is where Lua is genuinely
            unlike most languages.{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">A list and a record are the same kind of
            thing.</b>{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code={`{ "Ada", "Bo" }`}
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is shorthand for a table with keys 1 and 2. The numbers were keys all
            along.
          </p>

          <p>
            Which means one table can hold both at once, and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="#"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="ipairs"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            simply ignore the named keys. That is occasionally useful and more
            often confusing, so keep your lists and your records separate until
            you have a specific reason not to.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Walking a record</h3>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="pairs"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            visits <i>every</i> key, named or numbered. Its sibling{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="ipairs"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            only walks 1, 2, 3 and stops.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure2Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output — your order will differ"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Read that caption again: the order is not
            guaranteed.</b> It is not alphabetical, it is not the order you typed
            them, and it can differ between one Lua and another &mdash; the same
            file gives a different order in plain Lua than it does in LÖVE.
          </p>

          <p>
            So <b>never rely on it.</b> If you need a predictable order, collect
            the keys into a list, sort that, and walk the sorted list. It sounds
            laborious and it is four lines. You will write it in Task 3, and it
            is the standard Lua idiom rather than a workaround.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Records inside records</h3>

          <p>
            A value can be another table, all the way down. This is how you
            describe anything with structure.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure3Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>One caution.</b>{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="ship.bay.docked"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            only works if{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="ship.bay"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            actually exists. If it is nil, you are asking nil for a field, and{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">that finally is a real error</b> &mdash; one
            of the few things in Lua that stops your program rather than shrugging.
            Activity 1.12 covers what to do about it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Keys come and go</h3>

          <p>
            Records are not fixed shapes. Assigning to a new key adds it, and
            assigning{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="nil"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            removes it &mdash; there is no separate delete.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure4}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure4Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            "Set to nil" and "was never there" are indistinguishable, which is
            the price of not having a delete. It is also why{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="pairs"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            skips it afterwards &mdash; as far as the table is concerned, the key
            is gone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">
            The payoff: your globals were a table the whole time
          </h3>

          <p>
            Activity 1.2 warned you that leaving off{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="local"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            makes a variable global. Here is what that actually means.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure5}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure5Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Every global is a key in one ordinary table
            called{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="_G"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.</b> Writing{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="shipName = 'Skynest'"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is a table assignment. So is calling{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="print"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            &mdash; it is a lookup of the key{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="print"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            in{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="_G"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="string.format"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is a lookup of{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="format"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            in a table stored under{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="string"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two things fall out of that. A misspelled global returns nil because{" "}
            <b>you looked up a key that was not there</b> &mdash; it was never a
            special case. And globals are slower than locals because every use
            costs a table lookup, while a local is just a slot.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Do not write to{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="_G"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            on purpose.</b> It is here so the language stops looking like it has
            arbitrary rules. Nothing in Lua is a special case if you know it is
            tables the whole way down.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Start a fresh <b class="text-sn-theme">main.lua</b>. You are
            describing one ship properly, rather than with five loose variables
            like Activity 1.2 did.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Describe the ship</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Make one table with a <b>name</b>, a <b>crew</b> count, and an{" "}
              <b>online</b> flag.
            </li>
            <li>Print the name and the crew using dot access.</li>
            <li>
              Print{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="ship.captain"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />, which you never set. Confirm it is not an error.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="lua" title="main.lua" />

          <Code
            code={Task1Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Put a table inside it</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a <b>bay</b> field holding its own table with{" "}
              <b>docked</b> and <b>capacity</b>.
            </li>
            <li>Print both of those in one line.</li>
            <li>
              Dock one more ship by adding 1 to the nested field, then print it
              again.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "7, 14, 16-17" }}
          />

          <Code
            code={Task2Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Add a key, delete a key, list them in order</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Add a <b>charge</b> field after the fact and print it.</li>
            <li>
              Delete the <b>online</b> field by setting it to{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="nil"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </li>
            <li>
              Collect the remaining keys into a list, sort it, and print each key
              next to its{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="type()"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Do this with the sorted-keys idiom, not with bare{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="pairs"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /></b>{" "}
              &mdash; otherwise your output will not match, and it will not match
              differently on different machines. This task is Activities 1.9 and
              1.10 combined, which is the point.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "19-32" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Replace the bare{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="pairs"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              loop with one that prints only the fields that are <i>not</i>{" "}
              tables, so nested structures do not clutter the readout.
            </li>
            <li>
              Make a <i>list of ships</i>, each one a record, and print a line
              per ship. This is the shape almost every game's data has, and it is
              just Activity 1.9 holding Activity 1.10.
            </li>
            <li>
              Print{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="type(_G)"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              and{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="_G.print == print"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />. Both answers should now be unsurprising.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Check Your Work</h2>

          <p>Your finished file should produce this.</p>

          <Code
            code={Checkpoint}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            Four keys, alphabetical, and <b>online is not among them</b>. Note
            that <b class="text-sn-theme">bay reports as a table</b> &mdash; a
            field holding a whole other structure looks no different from the
            outside, which is the property the next activity builds on.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-5">
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-light"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-1/1-9"
          >
            <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Previous
          </a>
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-primary"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-1/1-11"
          >
            Next: Tables as Objects <i class="si-arrow-right"></i>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
